                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Protein per serving</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Cinnamon on label</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Sweetener</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Best for</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Ghost Whey Cinnabon</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">25 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Makara (cassia) cinnamon</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sucralose</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Best flavor</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Truvani Banana Cinnamon</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 20 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Organic Ceylon</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Monk fruit</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Daily use, vegan</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Quest Cinnamon Crunch</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">20 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Cinnamon (type not stated)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Erythritol, sucralose, stevia</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Low carb, baking</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">PEScience Snickerdoodle</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 24 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Flavoring only</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sucralose, ace-K</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Cookie flavor</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">ISO100 Vanilla + your cinnamon</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 25 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Your choice</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sucralose</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">DIY Ceylon</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Naked Whey + your cinnamon</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 25 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Your choice</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">None</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Unsweetened</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Figures are from labels and listings at the time of writing and vary by tub size and label revision. "Cinnamon on label" means what the ingredient list says, which is the only way to know whether a powder contains the spice or a cinnamon-flavored extract, and, if you are lucky, which kind of cinnamon it is.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The Best Cinnamon Protein Powders, Ranked
          </h2>

          <p>
            These six are ranked on cinnamon source and type, protein per serving, sweeteners and value, from labels and listings at the time of writing rather than our own taste tests. Four are cinnamon-flavored powders and two are plain bases for adding your own Ceylon. Figures vary by tub size and label revision.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="real-cinnamon-vs-flavoring" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Real Cinnamon or Cinnamon Flavor? Reading the Label
          </h2>

          <p>
            Cinnamon protein powders come in three types. Some list ground cinnamon as an ingredient: Ghost Cinnabon names Cinnabon's Makara cinnamon powder, Quest Cinnamon Crunch lists cinnamon, and Truvani names organic Ceylon cinnamon. Some list only "natural flavors" or "natural and artificial flavors", which for cinnamon usually means an extract built around cinnamaldehyde, the compound that gives cinnamon its warm, sweet bite. And some use both.
          </p>

          <p>
            PEScience Snickerdoodle is an example of the flavoring approach: the ingredient listings we found name no cinnamon at all, so its cinnamon taste comes from the flavor system. That is not a flaw if you just want something that tastes like a snickerdoodle cookie. It matters if you were buying it for cinnamon itself, or if you want to know exactly what kind of cinnamon you are eating.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why most labels just say cinnamon</h3>

          <p>
            In the US, "cinnamon" on a label can legally mean any of several Cinnamomum species, and most cinnamon sold here is cassia, usually Indonesian Korintje or Vietnamese (Saigon) cassia, rather than Ceylon. Ceylon (Cinnamomum verum, sometimes listed as C. zeylanicum) costs several times more, and brands that pay for it nearly always say so. So a label that just says "cinnamon" is very likely cassia.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where cinnamon sits on the ingredient list also tells you how much is there. Spices nearly always fall in the "contains 2% or less of" group at the end, and that is fine: a little cinnamon goes a long way. A cinnamon powder that lists cinnamon high in the main ingredients would be unusual and worth checking against the coumarin math below.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you want certainty, buy a cinnamon-free base and add your own. A <a href="/best-vanilla-protein-powder/">vanilla protein powder</a> plus a jar of Ceylon from the spice aisle costs little and removes all the guesswork.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cassia-vs-ceylon" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Cassia vs Ceylon: The Coumarin Question
          </h2>

          <p>
            Cassia and Ceylon taste similar but differ in one way that matters if you consume cinnamon every day: coumarin. Coumarin is a natural compound that, at high intakes, can cause liver injury in sensitive people, and it is the reason European regulators have looked closely at cinnamon. Cassia contains a lot of it. Ceylon contains very little.
          </p>

          <p>
            Published measurements vary widely between samples, but cassia commonly contains several milligrams of coumarin per gram of spice, with some samples much higher. Ceylon typically contains only trace amounts, often hundreds of times less. Vietnamese (Saigon) cassia, prized for its strong flavor, tends to be at the high end.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Type</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Common US label</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Coumarin</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Taste</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
