                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Ceylon (C. verum)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Ceylon cinnamon, true cinnamon</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Trace amounts</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Mild, floral, sweet</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Indonesian cassia (Korintje)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Cinnamon</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">High</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Classic, warm</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Vietnamese / Saigon cassia</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Saigon cinnamon</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Often highest</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Strong, spicy</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Chinese cassia</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Cinnamon, cassia</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">High</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Bold, slightly bitter</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">The number to know</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Tolerable daily intake: 0.1 mg of coumarin per kg of body weight</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">The European Food Safety Authority set this limit, which works out to about 6 mg a day for a 60 kg adult and about 8 mg for an 80 kg adult. Germany's Federal Institute for Risk Assessment has noted that a single teaspoon of a high-coumarin cassia can be enough to reach or exceed that for some adults. Ceylon does not raise the same concern.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            The tolerable daily intake is set with a large safety margin and is meant to apply over a lifetime, so going over it occasionally is not an emergency. The concern is habitual heavy intake, such as a heaped teaspoon of cassia in a daily shake plus cinnamon oatmeal plus cinnamon supplements. That is exactly the pattern of someone who loves cinnamon protein, which is why Ceylon is worth paying for if you add your own.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="coumarin-per-scoop" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How Much Coumarin Is in a Cinnamon Protein Shake?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Less than you might fear from a ready-made powder, and more than you might expect from a heavy-handed DIY shake. Spices in protein powder sit in the under-2% part of the ingredient list. Even at that 2% ceiling, a 34 g scoop would hold under 0.7 g of cinnamon. If that were a typical cassia, it would carry roughly 2 mg of coumarin, about a third of a 60 kg adult's daily limit, and in practice most powders use less than the ceiling.
          </p>

          <p>
            Brands do not disclose coumarin, so that is an upper-bound estimate, not a measurement. But it puts the ready-made powders in perspective: one scoop a day of a cassia-cinnamon whey is unlikely to be a problem for a healthy adult. Two or three scoops a day, plus other cinnamon foods, is where the numbers start to add up.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The DIY shake is where coumarin climbs</h3>

          <p>
            The bigger risk is adding your own. A level teaspoon of ground cinnamon weighs around 2.5 g. If that is cassia, you could be at or above the daily limit from one shake, and many recipe blogs call for a teaspoon or more. Use Ceylon for anything you eat daily, or keep cassia to a quarter teaspoon. Flavor-only powders such as PEScience Snickerdoodle contribute little or no coumarin, since the taste comes from extracts rather than bark.
          </p>

          <p>
            People with liver disease, anyone taking medication processed by the liver, and anyone pregnant should be more careful with cassia and should ask their doctor before using cinnamon supplements or large daily amounts. Children have lower body weight, so their limit is lower too; a cinnamon whey is not a children's drink anyway, as our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-kids/">protein powder for kids</a> guide explains.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="diy-cinnamon" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. How to Add Cinnamon to Protein Powder
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ground cinnamon does not dissolve; it is fine bark dust that floats on top of a shake and sticks to the sides. The fix is to put cinnamon in with the protein before the liquid, or blend. In a shaker, add milk first, then the protein and cinnamon together, then shake hard. A quarter to half a teaspoon is plenty for one shake.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cinnamon pairs naturally with vanilla, banana, coffee and oats. A cinnamon roll shake is vanilla whey, a quarter teaspoon of Ceylon and a pinch of salt in cold milk. Cinnamon banana bread is a frozen banana blended with vanilla or unflavored protein and cinnamon; our <a href="/best-banana-protein-powder/">banana protein powder</a> guide covers the banana side. A cinnamon latte is protein tempered into cool milk, then hot coffee, then cinnamon on top, as our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-coffee/">protein in coffee</a> guide explains.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cinnamon protein oatmeal</h3>

          <p>
            Cook the oats, let them cool for a minute, then stir in protein powder and cinnamon. Adding whey to boiling oats makes it clump and go rubbery. Cinnamon whey or a vanilla base with Ceylon both work; whey-casein blends like Quest and PEScience make thicker, creamier oats. Our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-oatmeal/">protein powder for oatmeal</a> guide has the full method.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cinnamon oil and very strong cinnamon extracts are a different thing from ground spice. Do not add cinnamon oil to shakes: it is highly concentrated, can irritate the mouth and stomach, and is not meant to be consumed in drops the way some online recipes suggest.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="blood-sugar-and-lead" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Does Cinnamon Protein Help Blood Sugar? And What About Lead?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Cinnamon is often marketed for blood sugar, and research has looked at it for decades. Meta-analyses of trials in people with type 2 diabetes have found modest reductions in fasting glucose in some analyses, with inconsistent results and studies of mixed quality, mostly using 1 to 6 g a day, often of cassia. The small amount of cinnamon in a scoop of protein powder is far below most of those study doses.
          </p>

          <p>
            So do not buy a cinnamon protein powder expecting it to lower your blood sugar, and do not raise your cinnamon intake to reach study doses without medical advice, given the coumarin issue. If you manage diabetes, choose a low-sugar powder for the protein and fiber benefits, and see our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-diabetics/">protein powder for diabetics</a> guide. Medication and targets belong with your doctor.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Lead in ground cinnamon</h3>

          <p>
            In 2023 and 2024 the FDA issued public health alerts about ground cinnamon contaminated with lead, first in recalled applesauce pouches and then in several ground cinnamon brands sold mainly at discount stores. That does not mean all cinnamon is contaminated, but it is a reason to buy cinnamon from established spice brands and to prefer protein brands that test finished products for heavy metals.
          </p>

          <p>
            For protein powder in general, the lead question is more about the protein source than the flavor. Plant proteins tend to test higher than whey. Our <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-heavy-metals/">protein powder without heavy metals</a> guide covers the published testing, and our <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">is protein powder safe</a> guide puts the numbers in context.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buying-on-amazon" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Buying Cinnamon Protein Powder on Amazon: What to Check
          </h2>

          <p>
            Cinnamon flavors are often licensed collaborations, such as Cinnabon or cinnamon cereal flavors, or seasonal fall releases. Those come and go, and when they go, third-party sellers keep listing leftover stock, sometimes well above the original price. Check "Ships from" and "Sold by" under the buy box and buy from the brand's own store or Amazon. A reseller listing for a discontinued flavor is the most likely place to get an old, heat-damaged tub.
          </p>

          <p>
            Read the label image for the cinnamon itself. Bullet points will say "real cinnamon" or "cinnamon roll flavor", but only the ingredient panel tells you whether cinnamon is an ingredient, whether it is Ceylon, and what sweetens the powder. If the label photo is too small to read, look for a second image, check the brand's site, or ask in the questions section. A brand using Ceylon will almost always say so, because it costs more.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check the lot and expiry date on arrival. Ground spices lose aroma over time, and a cinnamon powder that sat in a warehouse for a year will taste flatter. Photograph the date so a return is easy if it arrives close to expiry or with a broken seal.
          </p>

          <p>
            Watch the variation trap. The price in search results is often for the most popular flavor in the smallest size, not the cinnamon flavor and size you click into. Select the exact flavor and size, then work out cost per 25 g of protein: price divided by servings, divided by protein per serving, times 25. Quest's 20 g scoop and Ghost's 25 g scoop look similar on price per tub but not per gram. Our <a href="/tools/protein-cost-calculator/">protein cost calculator</a> does the math.
          </p>

          <p>
            Subscribe & Save typically takes 5% off, rising to as much as 15% when five or more subscriptions arrive in the same month. It is good value on a flavor you already know you like, and a bad idea on a limited flavor that may be swapped or discontinued mid-subscription. Our <a href="/amazon-protein-powder/">Amazon protein powder guide</a> covers seller checks for every flavor.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. How We Picked
          </h2>

          <p>
            We ranked cinnamon powders on whether cinnamon is a listed ingredient and which kind, protein per serving and per calorie, sweetener system, cost per gram of protein, and availability on Amazon US from the brand or Amazon. We did not run taste panels or lab tests for coumarin; flavor notes summarize buyer reports, and nutrition figures come from labels and listings at the time of writing. See <a href="/how-we-test/">how we test</a> for the method.
          </p>

          <p>
            We included two plain bases because adding your own Ceylon is the best way to control coumarin. We left out cinnamon flavors we could not confirm are currently sold on Amazon US. If the sweetener matters more to you than the spice, our <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-stevia/">protein powder without stevia</a> guide sorts powders by sweetener.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Adding a heaped teaspoon of cassia every day</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Supermarket cinnamon is almost always cassia, and a teaspoon of a high-coumarin cassia can reach a typical adult's tolerable daily limit on its own. Use Ceylon for anything you eat daily, or keep cassia to about a quarter teaspoon per shake.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming cinnamon flavor means cinnamon</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Some cinnamon-named powders contain no cinnamon on the ingredient list, only flavors. That is fine for taste, but if you wanted the spice itself, or wanted to know which kind, read the ingredient panel on the label image rather than the product name.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a cinnamon protein for blood sugar</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The cinnamon in a scoop is far below the 1 to 6 g used in blood sugar studies, and those studies show modest, inconsistent effects. Choose a low-sugar powder for diabetes, and leave medication and targets to your doctor.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Stirring cinnamon into a finished shake</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Ground cinnamon floats and clumps on top of liquid. Add it with the protein before shaking, or blend. Never add cinnamon oil to a shake, because it is highly concentrated and can irritate the mouth and stomach.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying a reseller premium for a limited flavor</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Licensed and seasonal cinnamon flavors are often discontinued, and resellers keep listing old stock at a markup. Buy from the brand or Amazon, check the date on arrival, and switch to a vanilla base plus Ceylon if the flavor is gone.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What is the best cinnamon protein powder?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  For most people, Ghost Whey Cinnabon, which gives 25 g of protein in about 130 calories and lists real Makara cinnamon. Truvani Banana Cinnamon is the best pick with Ceylon cinnamon, Quest Cinnamon Crunch is best for low carb and baking, and vanilla whey isolate plus your own Ceylon gives the most control over the spice.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is cassia cinnamon in protein powder safe?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  At the small amounts in a typical scoop, cassia is unlikely to be a problem for healthy adults. Coumarin becomes a concern with habitual heavy intake, such as a teaspoon of cassia in a daily shake plus other cinnamon foods. People with liver disease, on liver-processed medication or pregnant should ask their doctor.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What is the difference between Ceylon and cassia cinnamon?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Ceylon (Cinnamomum verum) is milder and more floral and contains only trace coumarin. Cassia, the common supermarket cinnamon from Indonesia, Vietnam or China, is stronger and contains much more coumarin. US labels usually just say cinnamon for cassia; brands that use Ceylon nearly always name it.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>How much cinnamon can I add to a protein shake?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  A quarter to half a teaspoon is enough for flavor. If you use cassia daily, keep to about a quarter teaspoon, since a full teaspoon of a high-coumarin cassia can reach the tolerable daily intake for many adults. With Ceylon, a half teaspoon a day is not a coumarin concern.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does cinnamon protein powder lower blood sugar?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Not in any meaningful way. Trials of cinnamon for blood sugar used 1 to 6 g a day and found modest, inconsistent effects, while a scoop of protein contains a fraction of a gram. A low-sugar protein can help a diabetes-friendly diet, but decisions about blood sugar management belong with your doctor.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is there a cinnamon protein powder without stevia?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Yes. Ghost Whey Cinnabon is sweetened with sucralose only, and Truvani Banana Cinnamon uses monk fruit only. Quest Cinnamon Crunch does contain stevia alongside erythritol and sucralose. For no sweetener at all, add Ceylon cinnamon to an unflavored whey such as Naked Whey.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Can I use cinnamon protein powder in oatmeal and baking?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Yes, and it works well. Stir it into oats after they cool slightly so the whey does not clump, and replace no more than about a third of the flour in bakes. Whey and casein blends such as Quest and PEScience stay moister in baking than pure isolates.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

          </div>
        </div>

